IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: Criminal Appeal No. 144-145 of 2004
CITATION: (2005) 6 SCC 1
YEAR: 2005

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: R.C. Lahoti, G.P. Mathur, P.K. Balasubramanyan

PARTIES:
Jacob Mathew vs State of Punjab

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Indian Penal Code, 1860

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Section 304A IPC

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
medical negligence, bolam test, gross negligence standard, doctors protection

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Established that criminal prosecution of doctors under Section 304A IPC requires gross negligence or recklessness, substantiated by independent medical opinion.

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Section 304A IPC.

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

